nss_qdisc: zero the dump structs before copying them out

Every nss_qdisc dump builds its reply in an uninitialised stack object and
hands the whole object to userspace:

	struct tc_nsstbl_qopt opt;

	opt.rate = q->rate;
	...
	if (opts == NULL || nla_put(skb, TCA_NSSTBL_PARMS, sizeof(opt), &opt)) {

Whatever the padding holds goes out with it. Seven of the uapi structs
carry padding the dumps never write: tc_nsstbl_qopt, tc_nssprio_qopt and
tc_nsshtb_qopt three bytes each, tc_nssfifo_qopt two, and
tc_nsswred_qopt, tc_nsscodel_qopt and tc_nssbf_qopt one - all of it from
a __u8 accel_mode following __u32 members. nss_wred_dump() additionally
leaves members unassigned on the branch it does not take.

This needs no privilege. `tc qdisc show` is RTM_GETQDISC, and
rtnetlink_rcv_msg() applies its CAP_NET_ADMIN test only to kinds other
than RTNL_KIND_GET, so any local process reads the bytes.

Found by a coverage-guided ASan harness over the qdisc netlink surface,
which reached three of them; the rest are the same shape and are fixed
together rather than left as siblings waiting to be found separately.

Zero each struct where it is declared, which is what the in-tree qdiscs
do for the same hazard - see sch_htb.c's tc_htb_glob dump. Applied
uniformly, including to the structs that have no padding today, because
whether a given one leaks is a property of the field list and a member
added later would reintroduce it silently.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/nss_qdisc/nss_bf.c
+++ b/nss_qdisc/nss_bf.c
@@ -506,6 +506,7 @@
 	struct nss_bf_class_data *cl = (struct nss_bf_class_data *)arg;
 	struct nlattr *opts;
 	struct tc_nssbf_class_qopt qopt;
+	memset(&qopt, 0, sizeof(qopt));
 
 	nss_qdisc_info("Dumping class %px of Qdisc %px\n", cl, sch);
 
@@ -819,6 +820,7 @@
 	struct nss_bf_sched_data *q = qdisc_priv(sch);
 	struct tc_nssbf_qopt qopt;
 	struct nlattr *opts = NULL;
+	memset(&qopt, 0, sizeof(qopt));
 
 	nss_qdisc_info("In bf dump qdisc\n");
 	qopt.defcls = q->defcls;
